TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "lineup coverage",
    "structural roles: organizer, shaper, terminal",
    "within-team variation",
    "talent",
    "role classification",
    "lineup composition"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to measure lineup coverage reliably from play-by-play derived role labels?",
    "How to isolate the effect of lineup coverage from the overall talent of the players on the floor?",
    "How to avoid lookahead bias when assigning roles to players for within-season stints?",
    "What is the statistical power given within-team stint-level variation and possible sparsity of uncovered lineups?",
    "What confounding pathways link role classification changes to team offensive performance beyond talent?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No prior studies on this exact question were found in the project's retrieval system."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No evidence is available on the separability of lineup coverage from talent, the extent of within-team variation, or the behaviour of talent controls."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "Lineup coverage can be operationalised as a binary indicator for each stint: at least one organizer, one shaper, and one terminal scorer simultaneously on the floor.",
    "Roles can be derived from the project's structural influence methodology using play-type initiation records, but temporal assignment must avoid future information.",
    "Within-team variation exists because coaches mix lineups; however, the proportion of stints with missing coverage needs to be checked in descriptive analysis.",
    "Talent can be measured by external player ratings (e.g., prior-season RPM) or by the number of each role type on the floor; the design must test whether coverage adds predictive power beyond these controls.",
    "The phrase 'talent control behaves' likely refers to the expectation that the coefficient of the talent control will be sensible and that the coverage effect will be robust to alternative talent specifications."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definition of 'talent control behaves' is ambiguous; it may refer to a placebo test or to checking the impact of multicollinearity.",
    "The stability of role labels within a season is unknown; players may change roles, causing measurement error in coverage.",
    "The sparsity of uncovered stints (lineups missing at least one of the three roles) is not yet known and could limit power.",
    "No historical power estimates are available, so the minimum detectable effect size for stint-level PPP is uncertain."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "The retrieval returned no earlier study-family records; therefore the decision to proceed with changes is based entirely on the methodological assessment of the data and the question."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.8,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
        "text": "Using whole-season or future data to assign player roles would leak information; this can be overcome by defining roles with a rolling lookback window updated before each game."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Player-level role classification (organizer, shaper, terminal) is subject to noise, especially for low-minute players, which may attenuate the estimated coverage effect."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "If most stints have full coverage, the variation in the key independent variable will be small, reducing power and necessitating sensitivity analyses using alternative coverage thresholds."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior study families were retrieved that substantially addressed the same question."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "Player roles assigned from the full season would use future information. Mitigation: roles must be recomputed daily using only data from the current season up to the previous game."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The structural role classifier may mislabel players, particularly those near the usage or residual thresholds; this misclassification error will attenuate the estimated effect of coverage and reduce power."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "If the proportion of stints lacking coverage (missing an organizer, shaper, or terminal) is very low, the effective sample size for the contrast will be small, potentially making the test underpowered."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Better players are more likely to occupy organizer, shaper, or terminal roles, so coverage may simply proxy for cumulative talent. The design must control for a composite talent metric to separate the influence of role composition from player quality."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Stint-level analyses with team fixed effects will require sufficient within-team variation; the effective number of independent stints per team may be limited, and the minimum detectable effect size should be estimated prior to analysis."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "If the analysis considers multiple outcomes (e.g., different play types, half-by-half splits) or multiple coverage definitions, multiplicity adjustments will be required to control false discovery."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.3,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.1,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.3
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The effect of a covered lineup (binary indicator that at least one organizer, one shaper, and one terminal scorer are simultaneously on the floor) on points per possession (PPP) during a stint, controlling for the composite talent of the five players on the floor.",
    "unit": "A stint (lineup interval) during an NBA game, defined by the team's offensive possession outcomes while that lineup is on the floor.",
    "sample": "All non-garbage-time stints from NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular seasons and playoffs, excluding stints shorter than two offensive possessions. Garbage time defined as the last two minutes of the fourth quarter (or overtime) when the score difference exceeds 15 points.",
    "power": "Power will be assessed using data from the 2022-23 and 2023-24 seasons as a pilot; the minimum detectable effect size will be computed for within-team variation in coverage, with the expectation that at least 90 team-seasons provide adequate power for an effect of 0.02 PPP (≈3 points per 100 possessions) at 80% power.",
    "controls": "1) Team-season fixed effects to isolate within-team variation. 2) A composite talent metric defined as the sum of each player's single-number offensive metric (e.g., prior-season Offensive Real Plus-Minus or, if unavailable, a player fixed-effect estimate from a prior-season regression). 3) Counts of organizers, shapers, and terminals on the floor to separate the binary coverage effect from the sheer quantity of each role type.",
    "falsification": "1) Test that lineup coverage is not associated with opponent offensive PPP (a null effect for defensive performance). 2) Randomly permute coverage labels within each team-season to confirm that the observed effect disappears. 3) Assess whether the coverage effect is robust to excluding games where a key starter is missing (selection due to injury).",
    "multiplicity": "Primary test: two-sided p < 0.05 on the coverage coefficient in the full model, with standard errors clustered by game. All additional splits (by play type, half, etc.) will use Benjamini-Hochberg correction. Sensitivity analyses with alternative talent metrics will not count against multiplicity.",
    "decision_rule": "Reject the null hypothesis of no effect if the coverage coefficient is positive and statistically significant at the corrected alpha level. If the coefficient is not significant but the confidence interval includes effects larger than 0.03 PPP, the result will be considered inconclusive.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 (through playoffs, using partial 2025-26 regular season only as a supplementary holdout). Roles will be recomputed with a lookback window ending on the day before each game, using only completed games."
  }
}

